# Two-agent workflow (Author + Reviewer)

Substantial coding work uses **two explicit roles**. Separate implementation from critique and repair.

## Agent 1 — Author (write code)

- Implement the requested change: minimal scope, match local conventions, correct types and APIs.
- Do not spend the same message “reviewing yourself” at length; ship the implementation.

## Agent 2 — Reviewer (code review and fix)

After Author output (follow-up message or explicit handoff):

- **Review** for correctness, edge cases, error handling, security (secrets, unsafe patterns), performance, and consistency with surrounding code.
- **Fix** issues you find: apply patches, don’t only list problems.
- Prefer running project linters/tests when available; if they can’t run, still fix obvious issues from static review.
- End with a **short delta summary**: what Reviewer changed vs what Author delivered.

## Using this in Cursor

- **Default:** User completes Author pass, then asks for a **Reviewer** pass on the same change (e.g. “Review and fix what you just wrote” or tag the touched files).
- **Single reply:** If the user asks to “implement and then review in one go,” do **Author first**, then **Reviewer** in clearly labeled sections within the same response, then apply Reviewer fixes before finishing.

Tiny edits (e.g. typo, rename in one place) may use a single pass unless the user requests full review.
